{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>When a man burns a field or vineyard.</b> An expression of burning and consuming, as in ואתה תבער הדם הנקי, and ובערתי את בית ירבעם כאשר יבער הגלל עד תומו — that he burns the field and the vineyard of the produce within them and destroys it." "<b>The best of his field.</b> Rashi: Scripture teaches you that damages are assessed at the best quality. The reason is that a person prefers his own kav more than the kavs of his fellow. And furthermore, his own is readily available to him. And furthermore, in order to warn him not to damage his fellow; but in the case of lender and borrower, he does not intend to harm him, but rather due to his distress he does not pay him fully — it suffices if he pays lowest-quality land; and so as not to lock the door before poor borrowers, the Sages enacted that he pay middle-quality land; and a woman's ketubah is at lowest quality, for he did not cause him any loss." "<b>The best of his field.</b> That which his field and vineyard were worthy of attaining their best if the animal had left it alone at the outset — the one who caused the damage pays the victim at the time of harvest or at the time of grape-gathering, for had it not been destroyed it would have grown and improved."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:44.451747790Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}